00:31:53.408 --> 00:31:56.798
are what makes Xennials feel
like they don't connect,

00:31:56.798 --> 00:31:59.830
'cause millennials, of
course it's not safe,

00:31:59.830 --> 00:32:01.480
they knew that from the beginning,

00:32:01.480 --> 00:32:02.505
9/11 happened when they were kids,

00:32:02.505 --> 00:32:06.901
they've grown up in the
midst of a never ending war,

00:32:06.901 --> 00:32:09.260
so violence is just an accepted part

00:32:09.260 --> 00:32:10.390
of the way the world works.

00:32:10.390 --> 00:32:13.730
But also, that violence and
exposure to it is enhanced

00:32:13.730 --> 00:32:16.048
because of the connectivity
that they had as kids,

00:32:16.048 --> 00:32:18.263
they got cell phones as kids,
they're on the internet,

00:32:18.263 --> 00:32:20.780
they're fluent in internet connectivity

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:21.620
from the very beginning,

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:23.370
so they see it all happening.
They have friends

00:32:23.370 --> 00:32:25.253
in other countries.
Right.

00:32:25.253 --> 00:32:26.700
Because of video games.
And we

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:29.972
may have had a pen pal through school,

00:32:29.972 --> 00:32:32.160
but they didn't do that
at Buies Creek Elementary.

00:32:32.160 --> 00:32:33.923
And it does make me think,

00:32:36.244 --> 00:32:39.340
is there a pre and post-COVID thing

00:32:39.340 --> 00:32:41.096
that's gonna happen with the.

00:32:41.096 --> 00:32:41.929
Alphas?

00:32:41.929 --> 00:32:45.720
Well, with our, what are our kids called?

00:32:45.720 --> 00:32:46.630
Zs.

00:32:46.630 --> 00:32:49.690
Yeah, Generation Z.

00:32:49.690 --> 00:32:54.580
Yeah, I'm sure that's impacted
them in some way, yeah,

00:32:54.580 --> 00:32:56.920
because, hate to break it to you,

00:32:56.920 --> 00:33:01.920
but COVID-19 is just the
beginning of global pandemics

00:33:02.654 --> 00:33:05.723
happening on a regular basis.

00:33:07.547 --> 00:33:09.763
And unfortunately, there's just,

00:33:09.763 --> 00:33:12.610
I was gonna get into the
climate change of it all,

00:33:12.610 --> 00:33:16.948
but it's just not acute enough
to speak of it that way.

00:33:16.948 --> 00:33:17.920
Yeah.

00:33:17.920 --> 00:33:19.666
It is for some people in some places.

00:33:19.666 --> 00:33:20.590
Right.

00:33:20.590 --> 00:33:22.840
Dependent on the
connections that you make,

00:33:23.940 --> 00:33:26.526
yeah, but for COVID, I
mean, I think about Lando,

00:33:26.526 --> 00:33:29.093
the youngest of both of our kids,

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:32.543
as an 11 year old,

00:33:34.600 --> 00:33:39.600
he's gonna process things as,

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:43.080
I know what it was like for
things to be taken away,

00:33:43.080 --> 00:33:45.631
I know what it was like for
my school to be taken away,

00:33:45.631 --> 00:33:47.860
for my friends to be taken away

00:33:50.464 --> 00:33:52.693
and he's rolled with it,

00:33:54.087 --> 00:33:56.710
but it's absolutely gonna stick with him.

00:33:56.710 --> 00:33:57.543
Oh yeah.

00:33:57.543 --> 00:34:01.750
And for that to happen when you're a kid,

00:34:01.750 --> 00:34:03.380
things impact you

00:34:03.380 --> 00:34:04.213
in a completely different way.
It's just like oh,

00:34:07.330 --> 00:34:10.450
for kids in 9/11, it's like oh,

00:34:10.450 --> 00:34:12.925
the world is a dangerous place,

00:34:12.925 --> 00:34:16.810
and it's a replay of that.

00:34:16.810 --> 00:34:18.163
But it still felt,

00:34:21.220 --> 00:34:22.840
9/11 still felt distant, though,

00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:25.572
as somebody who was in
rural North Carolina

00:34:25.572 --> 00:34:28.398
it was like, well, this ain't
going to happen in Fuquay,

00:34:28.398 --> 00:34:30.750
but it felt

00:34:30.750 --> 00:34:31.657
too close for comfort.
I was working at IBM,

00:34:31.657 --> 00:34:32.803
at the time,

00:34:34.524 --> 00:34:36.110
the research triangle park,

00:34:36.110 --> 00:34:38.036
there was so much technology focused there

00:34:38.036 --> 00:34:40.805
in, kind of, a Silicon Valley kind of way,

00:34:40.805 --> 00:34:45.805
that there were active discussions
about not going to work

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:49.822
because it could be a terrorist target,

00:34:49.822 --> 00:34:53.905
so we were talking about
not going to the office

00:34:53.905 --> 00:34:55.530
for that reason

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:57.501
and it really changes your thinking.

00:34:57.501 --> 00:35:01.870
But, as a kid, I think it changes a lot,

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:06.870
I also think with the COVID
of it all, for our kids,

00:35:07.809 --> 00:35:12.581
also translates more
directly into politics.

00:35:12.581 --> 00:35:17.473
Of course, with Generation X,

00:35:18.327 --> 00:35:21.655
with the experience of 9/11,

00:35:21.655 --> 00:35:26.655
everything was much more
partisan, as a result of that.

00:35:26.745 --> 00:35:28.627
There was an understanding of politics

00:35:28.627 --> 00:35:30.591
through the lens of 9/11

00:35:30.591 --> 00:35:32.270
and now, I think, for our kids,

00:35:32.270 --> 00:35:33.295
there's an understanding of politics

00:35:33.295 --> 00:35:36.178
through the lens of COVID,

00:35:36.178 --> 00:35:39.030
because it's so partisan.

00:35:39.030 --> 00:35:39.863
Right.

00:35:39.863 --> 00:35:43.520
Versus, when we were growing up,

00:35:43.520 --> 00:35:46.400
politics was just, we
would have mock elections

00:35:46.400 --> 00:35:47.233
and it would be cool

00:35:47.233 --> 00:35:49.678
to pretend we were voting for president,

00:35:49.678 --> 00:35:53.256
but we didn't feel the impact

00:35:53.256 --> 00:35:58.066
of a decision the governor made

00:35:58.066 --> 00:36:01.277
have any direct impact
on you, as a person.

00:36:01.277 --> 00:36:02.420
And the combination of those things,

00:36:02.420 --> 00:36:04.765
the combination of feeling less safe

00:36:04.765 --> 00:36:08.070
and then the awareness

00:36:08.070 --> 00:36:10.960
of what contributes to feeling less safe

00:36:10.960 --> 00:36:13.380
because of the access to information.

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:17.325
I talked about it in my
deconstruction story,

00:36:17.325 --> 00:36:22.087
that the biggest thing that impacted me,

00:36:22.087 --> 00:36:23.980
and this is impacting lots of people

00:36:23.980 --> 00:36:24.990
who come from, sort of,

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:29.300
any kind of ideology, any
sort of religious ideology,

00:36:29.300 --> 00:36:30.133
political ideology,

00:36:30.133 --> 00:36:33.323
that you were raised in a
certain set of principles

00:36:33.323 --> 00:36:37.224
that may or may not
necessarily reflect the truth,

00:36:37.224 --> 00:36:39.720
but they are the truth, capital T,

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:41.879
to you and your community.

00:36:41.879 --> 00:36:45.290
And for most of human history,

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:49.560
you've been able to maintain an adherence

00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:51.742
to that capital T truth

00:36:51.742 --> 00:36:53.860
for your entire life,

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:55.812
without it being challenged or examined.

00:36:55.812 --> 00:36:57.315
So you point to the internet.

00:36:57.315 --> 00:36:59.589
Unless you go to get a graduate degree

00:36:59.589 --> 00:37:01.405
in a particular thing, but yeah.

00:37:01.405 --> 00:37:05.030
College was really that
place where it's like, well.

00:37:05.030 --> 00:37:06.627
And I'm talking less about college

00:37:06.627 --> 00:37:09.363
and I'm saying that now you get access

00:37:09.363 --> 00:37:11.025
to that level of information,

00:37:11.025 --> 00:37:13.609
just by going on the internet.

00:37:13.609 --> 00:37:15.340
We've got a friend who
did not go to college,

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:19.331
who I consider my most educated friend,

00:37:19.331 --> 00:37:24.331
because that information is
available online, at this point,

00:37:24.917 --> 00:37:26.166
you gotta be good at navigating that,

00:37:26.166 --> 00:37:27.984
but I think that

00:37:27.984 --> 00:37:30.010
that, sort of, was the beginning

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:32.003
of my personal deconstruction.

00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:35.790
But I think that that is what,

00:37:38.713 --> 00:37:40.330
I was doing some reading

00:37:40.330 --> 00:37:44.510
as a lot of people see millennials,

00:37:44.510 --> 00:37:47.093
and I have been guilty of this, on my own,

00:37:48.017 --> 00:37:50.732
millennials as entitled, right?

00:37:50.732 --> 00:37:52.943
They seem entitled,

00:37:53.870 --> 00:37:56.640
they expect too much and they
don't wanna work as hard,

00:37:56.640 --> 00:37:58.400
and I don't necessarily
think that that's true,

00:37:58.400 --> 00:37:59.950
but I think they have that reputation

00:37:59.950 --> 00:38:01.340
and I have found myself

00:38:01.340 --> 00:38:03.967
falling into believing those stereotypes.

00:38:03.967 --> 00:38:08.594
But I was reading this
article in Inc. magazine

00:38:08.594 --> 00:38:12.658
and, basically, this
person was explaining this

00:38:12.658 --> 00:38:14.089
from this perspective,

00:38:14.089 --> 00:38:15.440
"The connected world

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.411
has empowered millennials
to take ownership,

00:38:18.411 --> 00:38:20.390
to take ownership,

00:38:20.390 --> 00:38:23.240
Glassdoor and LinkedIn allow
ownership of one's career,

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:24.910
YouTube allows ownership of one's content,

00:38:24.910 --> 00:38:26.303
Instagram and Snapchat allow ownership

00:38:26.303 --> 00:38:27.640
of one's personal brand,

00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:29.776
Netflix allows ownership of
one's content consumption."

00:38:29.776 --> 00:38:31.470
You could go on and on,

00:38:31.470 --> 00:38:34.497
Spotify, you got your
own music, et cetera.

00:38:34.497 --> 00:38:35.735
"The internet has offered millennials

00:38:35.735 --> 00:38:38.043
personalization and customization

00:38:38.043 --> 00:38:39.233
at every turn of their lives

00:38:39.233 --> 00:38:41.992
and now they expect the
same control at work

00:38:41.992 --> 00:38:43.226
and in their careers."

00:38:43.226 --> 00:38:46.410
So if you, 'cause, again,

00:38:46.410 --> 00:38:47.830
if you go back to Generation X,

00:38:47.830 --> 00:38:51.033
they grew up where they
had three channels,

00:38:51.033 --> 00:38:54.860
you watch the movie that
your parents decided to rent,

00:38:54.860 --> 00:38:57.310
you may or may not have had any say in it,

00:38:57.310 --> 00:38:58.860
depending on the structure of your family.

00:38:58.860 --> 00:39:01.885
I remember getting 34 channels but, still.

00:39:01.885 --> 00:39:04.149
You had cable,

00:39:04.149 --> 00:39:07.403
brick house, cable, bathroom.

00:39:07.403 --> 00:39:08.899
Yeah, you didn't have cable?

00:39:08.899 --> 00:39:11.716
No, my parents got cable
when I went to college!

00:39:11.716 --> 00:39:13.915
I remember coming home
after my freshman year

00:39:13.915 --> 00:39:16.132
and they had gotten cable,
I was like, what is this?

00:39:16.132 --> 00:39:17.698
And then I was flipping
through the channels

00:39:17.698 --> 00:39:20.800
and I got to a channel that was scrambled.

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:21.633
You know what, Rhett?

00:39:21.633 --> 00:39:22.466
You had a brother.

00:39:22.466 --> 00:39:23.299
Let me explain this.
Okay, okay.

00:39:23.299 --> 00:39:25.330
I went back home.
I had cable,

00:39:25.330 --> 00:39:26.343
I had Nickelodeon.

00:39:26.343 --> 00:39:29.012
And there was a scrambled channel,

00:39:29.012 --> 00:39:32.140
and there was a pair of
boobies in the corner.

00:39:32.140 --> 00:39:32.973
Boobs in the corner?

00:39:32.973 --> 00:39:34.490
And it was scrambled,

00:39:34.490 --> 00:39:37.672
but I could hear the sounds of a woman.

00:39:37.672 --> 00:39:38.950
Okay.

00:39:38.950 --> 00:39:39.850
Period, that's it,

00:39:39.850 --> 00:39:40.862
I'm not gonna say what she was doing,

00:39:40.862 --> 00:39:43.180
but you can imagine.

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:47.083
And I was like, well, what is this?

00:39:48.374 --> 00:39:49.207
Who do I call

00:39:49.207 --> 00:39:50.533
to unscramble this?
This is when you came home

00:39:50.533 --> 00:39:51.366
from college?

00:39:51.366 --> 00:39:52.357
Yeah!

00:39:52.357 --> 00:39:54.015
And then I figured out

00:39:54.015 --> 00:39:59.015
that if you watch this particular
channel at a certain time,

00:39:59.633 --> 00:40:04.633
they would put on a movie
that was not family friendly

00:40:04.857 --> 00:40:06.263
and then they would scramble it,

00:40:06.263 --> 00:40:09.280
and sometimes the guy who was
making the scramble decision

00:40:09.280 --> 00:40:11.151
would be two minutes late,

00:40:11.151 --> 00:40:14.527
and you'd get two minutes
of unscrambled boobies.

00:40:14.527 --> 00:40:16.730
If they were the beginning of the movie.

00:40:16.730 --> 00:40:18.360
And they were pretty immediate

00:40:18.360 --> 00:40:20.260
with the particular movies
that I was watching.

00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:21.093
As a college freshmen.

00:40:21.093 --> 00:40:22.370
I think that was it,

00:40:22.370 --> 00:40:24.210
it was shortly after going off to college,

00:40:24.210 --> 00:40:27.057
they got cable and they got
the scrambled booby channel.

00:40:27.057 --> 00:40:30.416
Yeah, you talk about the level
of access now, it's like.

00:40:30.416 --> 00:40:33.560
Yeah, think about how
easy it is to see boobies

00:40:33.560 --> 00:40:35.253
if you wanna see 'em now, right,

00:40:36.175 --> 00:40:38.135
it is so different.

00:40:38.135 --> 00:40:43.135
And so I think, I've got into discussions,

00:40:44.100 --> 00:40:46.080
my dad is now retired,

00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:48.856
he was a law professor for many
years, he retired this year

00:40:48.856 --> 00:40:51.797
and I really enjoyed going home

00:40:51.797 --> 00:40:54.306
and talking to him about the students.

00:40:54.306 --> 00:40:57.988
Now, he had a reputation
for being a very difficult,

00:40:57.988 --> 00:41:00.163
but very good, professor,

00:41:01.730 --> 00:41:03.450
most students really liked him

00:41:03.450 --> 00:41:05.964
but if you were not on top
of it, you might hate him,

00:41:05.964 --> 00:41:08.600
because he was a difficult professor.

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.433
But he always,

00:41:09.433 --> 00:41:12.047
from the very first time
I ever talked to him

00:41:12.047 --> 00:41:15.791
about the exams that he would
be grading over Christmas

00:41:15.791 --> 00:41:18.319
or around the holidays,

00:41:18.319 --> 00:41:20.672
he was always complaining about,

00:41:20.672 --> 00:41:22.790
it didn't matter what year it was,

00:41:22.790 --> 00:41:25.255
he was always like, ah,
they just don't understand.

00:41:25.255 --> 00:41:28.570
And every year it felt like they just,

00:41:28.570 --> 00:41:29.610
they really don't understand,

00:41:29.610 --> 00:41:31.569
he was becoming an old man

00:41:31.569 --> 00:41:35.107
and his classes were staying the same age,

00:41:35.107 --> 00:41:37.036
and he was seeing the generations,

00:41:37.036 --> 00:41:41.878
through X and through
Xennials into millennials.

00:41:41.878 --> 00:41:44.938
And we had some interesting conversations,

00:41:44.938 --> 00:41:48.401
'cause I was like, yeah,
you might get frustrated

00:41:48.401 --> 00:41:53.401
that your students don't seem as committed

00:41:53.640 --> 00:41:56.100
to understanding this
particular thing in this way,

00:41:56.100 --> 00:41:59.990
or they don't seem as capable of this,

00:41:59.990 --> 00:42:00.823
but have you thought about

00:42:00.823 --> 00:42:03.210
how they can actually
see things in this way,

00:42:03.210 --> 00:42:06.217
because they're more connected
thinkers, or whatever.

00:42:06.217 --> 00:42:08.324
The generations just change,

00:42:08.324 --> 00:42:11.176
but I find myself sometimes
thinking like, yeah,

00:42:11.176 --> 00:42:12.790
they are entitled,

00:42:12.790 --> 00:42:14.997
they just think that they
should get whatever they want

00:42:14.997 --> 00:42:17.402
but you gotta understand, you
gotta gotta pay your dues,

00:42:17.402 --> 00:42:19.495
you gotta work for what you get.

00:42:19.495 --> 00:42:22.610
And I think this is an interesting theory,

00:42:22.610 --> 00:42:25.320
you live in a world of customization,

00:42:25.320 --> 00:42:26.939
where it's just my phone,

00:42:26.939 --> 00:42:28.720
if you pull up my phone

00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:31.670
it represents my tastes in everything,

00:42:31.670 --> 00:42:33.711
my digital world is curated,

00:42:33.711 --> 00:42:36.923
my apps are curated according to my tastes

00:42:36.923 --> 00:42:40.357
and that's a fascinating
way to think about now,

00:42:40.357 --> 00:42:42.660
how does that not transfer over

00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:45.100
into the way that you see the world?

00:42:45.100 --> 00:42:48.063
Yeah, absolutely, it ironically,

00:42:49.508 --> 00:42:52.770
and we talked about this recently,

00:42:52.770 --> 00:42:56.840
technology enables a level of connection,

00:42:56.840 --> 00:43:01.630
but then it's increasingly incentivized

00:43:01.630 --> 00:43:05.446
to set up a customized
experience, just for you,

00:43:05.446 --> 00:43:08.973
that creates insulation and isolation,

00:43:08.973 --> 00:43:13.973
and that's happening more and
more, algorithmically, right?

00:43:14.215 --> 00:43:16.782
We talked about "The Social
Dilemma" documentary,

00:43:16.782 --> 00:43:18.603
we don't have to go back there,

00:43:21.329 --> 00:43:23.410
the early internet,

00:43:23.410 --> 00:43:27.680
it was just more of volitional connection,

00:43:27.680 --> 00:43:30.025
now it's volitional isolation,

00:43:30.025 --> 00:43:33.083
because that's the internet
that we experienced,

00:43:35.554 --> 00:43:40.554
and then you realize the
ways you've been hoodwinked.

00:43:41.347 --> 00:43:43.847
Again, I'm just thinking
out loud at this point,

00:43:44.790 --> 00:43:46.362
becoming an adult

00:43:46.362 --> 00:43:49.722
before you had those opportunities

00:43:49.722 --> 00:43:53.824
for customization and isolation

00:43:53.824 --> 00:43:56.416
through your connectivity,

00:43:56.416 --> 00:44:01.416
'cause I think that Xennials
and even Generation Xers,

00:44:02.684 --> 00:44:04.483
and even boomers, for that matter,

00:44:04.483 --> 00:44:09.280
might be more susceptible to
addiction to their phones.

00:44:13.176 --> 00:44:15.823
I know everyone's kind of
addicted to their phones,

00:44:15.823 --> 00:44:18.493
but I don't necessarily know,

00:44:19.598 --> 00:44:21.325
'cause I feel like the older you are,

00:44:21.325 --> 00:44:23.143
the more it kind of snuck up on you.

00:44:23.143 --> 00:44:24.422
If you're a Generation Xer,

00:44:24.422 --> 00:44:28.780
it's not that you're not
completely consumed by your phone,

00:44:28.780 --> 00:44:30.108
it just means you can remember a time

00:44:30.108 --> 00:44:31.900
that you weren't,

00:44:31.900 --> 00:44:34.337
but does that mean that
you're less susceptible to it?

00:44:34.337 --> 00:44:36.700
Are our kids gonna have better tools

00:44:36.700 --> 00:44:38.610
to manage and to deal with?

00:44:38.610 --> 00:44:43.610
I was saying that every 43
year old plumber in America.

00:44:43.720 --> 00:44:45.255
Has an iPhone?

00:44:45.255 --> 00:44:49.579
Yeah, but they're not all
equally addicted to Facebook

00:44:49.579 --> 00:44:50.780
or Instagram.

00:44:50.780 --> 00:44:52.839
But that's why it's a cusp,

00:44:52.839 --> 00:44:54.200
there's a gradient

00:44:54.200 --> 00:44:58.550
and at 43, you're technically
into Generation X,

00:44:58.550 --> 00:45:00.125
and so you may not be,

00:45:00.125 --> 00:45:01.039
but I'm just saying

00:45:01.039 --> 00:45:05.180
that a very high percentage
of 43 year old plumbers

00:45:07.306 --> 00:45:11.130
have a curated digital experience,
just like anybody else.

00:45:11.130 --> 00:45:12.280
Yeah and I'm thinking

00:45:15.750 --> 00:45:20.193
the technology has such an
impact on who we are as people,

00:45:22.496 --> 00:45:27.496
it has defined these
generations in a lot of ways.

00:45:28.260 --> 00:45:30.710
I think about, even if
you look at something

00:45:30.710 --> 00:45:34.832
like our parents, trying
to teach our parents

00:45:34.832 --> 00:45:39.021
about using a phone, or
even using computers,

00:45:39.021 --> 00:45:40.368
they didn't have computers in the house,

00:45:40.368 --> 00:45:42.525
there was this distrust of it,

00:45:42.525 --> 00:45:44.348
I'm not gonna put my banking
information on there,

00:45:44.348 --> 00:45:46.286
I'm actually not even
gonna click on this thing,

00:45:46.286 --> 00:45:48.280
because what if it's messed up?

00:45:48.280 --> 00:45:51.094
And you're just like, anything
you click on, you can undo,

00:45:51.094 --> 00:45:52.849
they didn't even understand that,

00:45:52.849 --> 00:45:57.849
so there was this suspicion
associated with it.

00:45:59.700 --> 00:46:02.363
But then, with our generation,

00:46:03.667 --> 00:46:07.450
we were the ones who
had to make the decision

00:46:07.450 --> 00:46:08.636
to start trusting

00:46:08.636 --> 00:46:12.720
and to give more of ourselves
over to the internet,

00:46:12.720 --> 00:46:14.705
and now the generation after us

00:46:14.705 --> 00:46:16.766
reap the problems and the benefits

00:46:16.766 --> 00:46:20.584
of once you do give yourself
over to it completely,

00:46:20.584 --> 00:46:22.612
what can happen.

00:46:22.612 --> 00:46:24.298
And then there starts to be,

00:46:24.298 --> 00:46:27.285
I think there's now
another wave of, hopefully,

00:46:27.285 --> 00:46:29.770
pulling back and saying, okay,

00:46:29.770 --> 00:46:32.840
maybe I'm not gonna
accept all these cookies

00:46:32.840 --> 00:46:36.733
and I'm gonna know how
these ads are working,

00:46:38.496 --> 00:46:40.546
I'm gonna get beyond the magic of this

00:46:40.546 --> 00:46:44.833
in order to know what proper
boundaries are for myself.

00:46:46.700 --> 00:46:48.941
There's a level of skepticism

00:46:48.941 --> 00:46:51.433
that, I think, is coming back in now.

00:46:53.250 --> 00:46:57.175
Is that skepticism coming
from the millennials

00:46:57.175 --> 00:46:59.900
or the Zs themselves?

00:46:59.900 --> 00:47:01.094
I don't think so,

00:47:01.094 --> 00:47:05.510
I think if you watch "The Social Dilemma",

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:08.530
most of this pushback
and sort of gut check

00:47:08.530 --> 00:47:10.500
that's happening around social media.

00:47:10.500 --> 00:47:11.333
Is from us.

00:47:11.333 --> 00:47:13.300
It's happening from the
millennials and the Xennials,

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:16.610
and the Generation Xers who
developed the platforms.

00:47:16.610 --> 00:47:17.443
Yeah.

00:47:17.443 --> 00:47:20.310
And are seeing what's
happening to their children

00:47:20.310 --> 00:47:22.050
and getting very worried
about their own children,

00:47:22.050 --> 00:47:22.980
and just getting worried

00:47:22.980 --> 00:47:24.766
about the state of the world.

00:47:24.766 --> 00:47:27.020
I can't remember where I saw this,

00:47:27.020 --> 00:47:27.853
but somebody was talking

00:47:27.853 --> 00:47:30.454
about how there's this sort of trope

00:47:30.454 --> 00:47:34.877
that these Generation
Zers are so tech savvy,

00:47:34.877 --> 00:47:38.880
and this was a millennial
who was saying this,

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:40.610
and they were like, actually, they're not,

00:47:40.610 --> 00:47:43.554
being able to make a TikTok video

00:47:43.554 --> 00:47:46.868
and operate an app on your phone,

00:47:46.868 --> 00:47:50.025
is not tech savvy, it's app savvy.

00:47:50.025 --> 00:47:54.468
Tech savvy is knowing about
the underlying features

00:47:54.468 --> 00:47:56.420
of the thing that you're interacting with,

00:47:56.420 --> 00:47:58.403
knowing how to write
code, that kind of thing,

00:47:58.403 --> 00:48:00.662
obviously, that's happening
across all generations.

00:48:00.662 --> 00:48:03.620
But there was this
little bit of like, guys,

00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:06.210
they're not actually that tech savvy,

00:48:06.210 --> 00:48:07.043
they actually don't care

00:48:07.043 --> 00:48:09.592
about specifications and technology

00:48:09.592 --> 00:48:12.623
as much as people who are a
little bit older than them,

00:48:14.183 --> 00:48:19.183
their apps and their phone is
an extension of themselves.

00:48:19.248 --> 00:48:22.068
Think about the way that
you interact with your kids,

00:48:22.068 --> 00:48:25.363
if there's a computer issue at home,

00:48:27.114 --> 00:48:31.122
who addresses the computer
issue, you or your kids?

00:48:31.122 --> 00:48:32.531
Who is the most equipped

00:48:32.531 --> 00:48:36.950
to deal with something happening
with a computer at home?

00:48:36.950 --> 00:48:38.736
Lincoln started building his own computers

00:48:38.736 --> 00:48:43.736
and then he broke his computer,
he fried his motherboard,

00:48:44.570 --> 00:48:46.684
and I was in the room when he did it,

00:48:46.684 --> 00:48:48.585
it was quite a learning experience,

00:48:48.585 --> 00:48:51.980
the sound that he made when he realized

00:48:51.980 --> 00:48:56.980
that he had touched his
motherboard and fried it.

00:48:56.986 --> 00:48:58.941
Did it smoke?

00:48:58.941 --> 00:49:03.604
It didn't smoke, no, it just
ceased to work entirely.

00:49:03.604 --> 00:49:05.161
When was this?
And he was like,

00:49:05.161 --> 00:49:07.146
a month ago,

00:49:07.146 --> 00:49:09.689
and he looks at me,

00:49:09.689 --> 00:49:12.559
and I'm just like, I'm just
in here to pet the dog, dude,

00:49:12.559 --> 00:49:13.495
I got nothing.

00:49:13.495 --> 00:49:14.784
So he's building a PC?

00:49:14.784 --> 00:49:18.690
Yeah, like a gaming PC,

00:49:18.690 --> 00:49:21.563
but he had to figure that
out on his own, yeah,

00:49:23.219 --> 00:49:25.808
but I still had to teach him a little bit,

00:49:25.808 --> 00:49:27.947
you have to Google what just happened.

00:49:27.947 --> 00:49:32.947
I think I just fried my
motherboard, Google that, you know?

00:49:33.441 --> 00:49:38.006
Yeah, Locke almost got into
the computer building phase,

00:49:38.006 --> 00:49:40.750
I don't know if I encouraged
it or discouraged it,

00:49:40.750 --> 00:49:41.583
I don't remember,

00:49:41.583 --> 00:49:42.416
but he thought he was gonna do that,

00:49:42.416 --> 00:49:43.839
I was like, okay, good,

00:49:43.839 --> 00:49:45.523
just let me know when you want to do it,

00:49:45.523 --> 00:49:47.750
then it never came to fruition.

00:49:47.750 --> 00:49:49.437
Lincoln's saving all his money

00:49:49.437 --> 00:49:53.030
for the next component
now, that's what he's into.

00:49:53.030 --> 00:49:53.930
Okay, well.

00:49:53.930 --> 00:49:54.812
And I thought that was a good thing.

00:49:54.812 --> 00:49:56.053
Yeah, for sure.

00:49:56.980 --> 00:49:58.745
Because I can't help him

00:49:58.745 --> 00:50:01.579
and he's so into it, that he's
gotta figure it out himself,

00:50:01.579 --> 00:50:04.450
it's ironic because I worked for a company

00:50:04.450 --> 00:50:05.456
that built computers.

00:50:05.456 --> 00:50:06.919
When I worked for IBM

00:50:06.919 --> 00:50:10.238
and we would refurbish
every type of computer

00:50:10.238 --> 00:50:11.256
that they made,

00:50:11.256 --> 00:50:13.561
they would refurbish Dell computers

00:50:13.561 --> 00:50:17.299
and then resell them
to businesses, strange,

00:50:17.299 --> 00:50:20.900
laptops, servers, desktops, everything,

00:50:20.900 --> 00:50:23.402
even peripherals, peripherals.

00:50:23.402 --> 00:50:24.750
Did you learn anything?

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:27.111
Is that what you're getting at?

00:50:27.111 --> 00:50:29.893
I just designed the
conveyor belts and stuff,

00:50:30.913 --> 00:50:34.244
computers were still so new to me.

00:50:34.244 --> 00:50:36.263
I didn't understand,

00:50:38.479 --> 00:50:40.874
they gotta zap the ram and then they

00:50:40.874 --> 00:50:42.151
gotta clean the hard drive.
Gotta zap that ram.

00:50:42.151 --> 00:50:44.427
I had no clue what this stuff was.

00:50:44.427 --> 00:50:45.491
Well, I guess what I'm saying is,

00:50:45.491 --> 00:50:47.740
if you have an interaction
with your parents

00:50:47.740 --> 00:50:50.011
and I have an interaction with
my parents, about a computer,

00:50:50.011 --> 00:50:52.642
there is clearly someone who is the expert

00:50:52.642 --> 00:50:54.186
and someone who is not.

00:50:54.186 --> 00:50:55.180
Yeah.

00:50:55.180 --> 00:50:56.401
But when it comes to our kids,

00:50:56.401 --> 00:50:58.329
I just don't think it transfers,

00:50:58.329 --> 00:51:00.160
it's not as clean cut,

00:51:00.160 --> 00:51:00.993
it's not like.
No it's not.

00:51:00.993 --> 00:51:01.826
Oh, they're younger, so, therefore,

00:51:01.826 --> 00:51:02.659
they know more.
I thought that's

00:51:02.659 --> 00:51:04.233
what you were getting at, but you're not.

00:51:04.233 --> 00:51:07.260
I'm saying that's not the
case, that's one of the things

00:51:07.260 --> 00:51:11.100
We're still burdened with
IT issues, you and I,

00:51:11.100 --> 00:51:12.094
in our homes,

00:51:12.094 --> 00:51:14.210
is what you're saying?
Yes.

00:51:14.210 --> 00:51:15.589
Whereas, my dad'll call me

00:51:15.589 --> 00:51:19.990
and I literally talked
to him for 30 minutes

00:51:19.990 --> 00:51:23.905
about his his cable internet.
You know what it's like?

00:51:23.905 --> 00:51:28.176
When I was 16, 17, 18,
I got my first truck,

00:51:28.176 --> 00:51:31.082
I didn't know how to fix anything in it,

00:51:31.082 --> 00:51:33.689
but it seemed that my dad and my granddad

00:51:33.689 --> 00:51:36.459
knew how to fix everything with the car.

00:51:36.459 --> 00:51:38.871
And so if something
went wrong with the car,

00:51:38.871 --> 00:51:40.705
I'd be like, well, you're
supposed to take it to a shop

00:51:40.705 --> 00:51:41.760
and they're supposed to fix it,

00:51:41.760 --> 00:51:45.466
or we'd get a new one,
if it's too messed up.

00:51:45.466 --> 00:51:49.260
That's how our kids are
with computers and phones,

00:51:49.260 --> 00:51:50.963
my phone doesn't work anymore,

00:51:52.050 --> 00:51:57.050
well have you reset the
computer, have you restarted it?

00:51:57.090 --> 00:51:58.913
Have you wiped your phone clean?

00:52:00.096 --> 00:52:02.560
Things that we know how to do

00:52:02.560 --> 00:52:04.245
because we came up

00:52:04.245 --> 00:52:06.389
having to figure out all
of that stuff, ourselves.

00:52:06.389 --> 00:52:07.222
Just last week.

00:52:07.222 --> 00:52:09.101
They just want to throw
it away and get a new one.

00:52:09.101 --> 00:52:10.567
But our parents,

00:52:10.567 --> 00:52:13.940
our parents could fix cars.
Fix stuff, yeah.

00:52:13.940 --> 00:52:16.420
They could fix cars in the same way.

00:52:16.420 --> 00:52:17.302
So Locke did the same thing,

00:52:17.302 --> 00:52:20.324
he comes to me, my phone
screen is just blinking,

00:52:20.324 --> 00:52:21.574
it won't turn on,

00:52:21.574 --> 00:52:24.545
and, of course, he ended
up getting a replacement

00:52:24.545 --> 00:52:26.210
because we've got insurance on it.

00:52:26.210 --> 00:52:29.183
But the diagnosing of that issue

00:52:29.183 --> 00:52:31.350
and getting on the phone with tech support

00:52:31.350 --> 00:52:32.725
is Daddy's job, right.

00:52:32.725 --> 00:52:35.419
Again, I think that that's
a generational thing

00:52:35.419 --> 00:52:39.009
that my parents would have made
me figure it out, probably,

00:52:39.009 --> 00:52:41.360
and I probably should've
also done the same thing,

00:52:41.360 --> 00:52:43.783
but I digress.

00:52:46.490 --> 00:52:49.130
We had to learn how to change the oil,

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:50.980
you change the oil in
your car yourself, right?

00:52:50.980 --> 00:52:52.706
A couple times.
At least a couple of times.

00:52:52.706 --> 00:52:53.539
But the point

00:52:53.539 --> 00:52:55.718
you're getting at.
I change the oil in my truck

00:52:55.718 --> 00:52:56.551
all the time.

00:52:56.551 --> 00:52:57.732
There's an observation that somebody made,

00:52:57.732 --> 00:53:01.180
which is one of the things that has,

00:53:01.180 --> 00:53:02.508
and I don't know exactly
the cutoff for this,

00:53:02.508 --> 00:53:07.250
but for the majority of
human history your parents,

00:53:07.250 --> 00:53:09.060
the generation before you,

00:53:09.060 --> 00:53:09.893
was wiser

00:53:09.893 --> 00:53:14.313
about everything that you could
be wise about, everything,

00:53:15.475 --> 00:53:16.308
you know what I'm saying?

00:53:16.308 --> 00:53:20.143
You were basically an apprentice
to the next generation

00:53:20.143 --> 00:53:23.006
because technology was
introduced so slowly,

00:53:23.006 --> 00:53:25.004
there were certain exceptions,

00:53:25.004 --> 00:53:27.090
but it wasn't moving like it is right now,

00:53:27.090 --> 00:53:30.116
there were not these
seismic shifts in technology

00:53:30.116 --> 00:53:31.515
and the way that people live.

00:53:31.515 --> 00:53:34.950
And so, as a younger person,

00:53:34.950 --> 00:53:37.217
an older person had all the knowledge

00:53:37.217 --> 00:53:39.030
that you could ever hope to get

00:53:39.030 --> 00:53:40.915
was contained in a person
who was older than you,

00:53:40.915 --> 00:53:42.688
now.
You get an app

00:53:42.688 --> 00:53:43.892
and then all of a sudden,

00:53:43.892 --> 00:53:47.033
this app becomes, kind of,
the center of your life.

00:53:47.033 --> 00:53:49.020
Well, the source of the knowledge

00:53:49.020 --> 00:53:49.921
is not a person.
Just as an example.

00:53:49.921 --> 00:53:52.940
The source of knowledge is not people,

00:53:52.940 --> 00:53:56.180
the source of knowledge is the internet,

00:53:56.180 --> 00:53:58.570
it's something that you can access.

00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:00.020
And so that's why, again,

00:54:00.020 --> 00:54:00.853
I feel this way

00:54:00.853 --> 00:54:05.220
about the way that I
interact with my doctor,

00:54:05.220 --> 00:54:07.012
I respect my doctor

00:54:07.012 --> 00:54:10.310
and I think my doctor is
schooled and intelligent,

00:54:10.310 --> 00:54:11.818
and I'm not one of those
people that goes in

00:54:11.818 --> 00:54:13.141
and thinks that I know more

00:54:13.141 --> 00:54:16.160
about medical things, period, than you,

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:19.150
but there are times,
and I'm a hypochondriac,

00:54:19.150 --> 00:54:22.647
so if there's a specific
thing that I'm experiencing,

00:54:22.647 --> 00:54:26.727
I have this temptation to
believe, right or wrong,

00:54:26.727 --> 00:54:29.770
that I might know more, are you okay?

00:54:29.770 --> 00:54:31.150
Do I need to diagnose you?

00:54:31.150 --> 00:54:32.807
Do I need to take you to my doctor?

00:54:32.807 --> 00:54:35.553
I'm sorry.

00:54:38.430 --> 00:54:39.600
It looked like you threw
water into your mouth

00:54:39.600 --> 00:54:40.879
and forgot to swallow,

00:54:40.879 --> 00:54:43.670
that was my experience of what happened,

00:54:43.670 --> 00:54:46.082
it was like this.
I'm sorry,

00:54:46.082 --> 00:54:49.810
I coughed and then water came out my nose,

00:54:49.810 --> 00:54:52.749
I'm glad I don't have a deviated septum.

00:54:52.749 --> 00:54:54.781
Actually, it could
protect you, in that case.

00:54:54.781 --> 00:54:57.772
I'm just gonna wipe my face.

00:54:57.772 --> 00:54:59.727
As I was trying to say.

00:54:59.727 --> 00:55:02.753
Did I break your concentration?

00:55:03.620 --> 00:55:04.786
Oh gosh.
I tend to think

00:55:04.786 --> 00:55:08.625
that I can know more about a
specific thing than my doctor,

00:55:08.625 --> 00:55:11.490
because I'm like, I don't know,

00:55:11.490 --> 00:55:13.228
I don't know if he's done
this particular research

00:55:13.228 --> 00:55:15.540
that I just did.

00:55:15.540 --> 00:55:17.584
And I could be wrong
about that, of course,

00:55:17.584 --> 00:55:19.714
and I'm sure it's annoying, at times,

00:55:19.714 --> 00:55:24.714
but that's why your kids
can seem more knowledgeable

00:55:26.816 --> 00:55:28.587
and smarter than you.

00:55:28.587 --> 00:55:31.090
And the point that the
same article is making

00:55:31.090 --> 00:55:36.090
is that it creates this
interesting sort of contradiction,

00:55:36.620 --> 00:55:38.805
because, as a millennial
or as a Generation Zer,

00:55:38.805 --> 00:55:41.413
you can have access to this knowledge,

00:55:41.413 --> 00:55:43.970
but what you don't have,

00:55:43.970 --> 00:55:45.650
is you don't have the lived experience

00:55:45.650 --> 00:55:47.140
that somebody older than you has,

00:55:47.140 --> 00:55:48.162
but you have the knowledge that they don't

00:55:48.162 --> 00:55:49.495
about a particular thing.

00:55:49.495 --> 00:55:51.415
That creates this weird dynamic,

00:55:51.415 --> 00:55:53.915
especially between
employers and employees,

00:55:53.915 --> 00:55:55.273
'cause you're like, okay,

00:55:55.273 --> 00:55:59.502
as an employer, technically
have authority over you,

00:55:59.502 --> 00:56:01.382
to a certain degree,

00:56:01.382 --> 00:56:04.026
I can say that you get fired,
or I can say who gets hired,

00:56:04.026 --> 00:56:06.484
I can change your job
description, or whatever.

00:56:06.484 --> 00:56:09.253
But you're coming from the perspective,

00:56:11.469 --> 00:56:13.430
this is the case with us,

00:56:13.430 --> 00:56:16.061
it's like by design,

00:56:16.061 --> 00:56:18.289
most of the people who work for us

00:56:18.289 --> 00:56:21.090
know more about the thing
that they're doing for us

00:56:21.090 --> 00:56:23.413
than we do, right, by design.

00:56:25.608 --> 00:56:27.170
Yeah.

00:56:27.170 --> 00:56:29.090
But we're older, we've
been doing this for longer,

00:56:29.090 --> 00:56:30.600
and we're technically their boss,

00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:32.287
so it just creates this,

00:56:32.287 --> 00:56:35.605
and I don't think it's that
big of a deal in our company,

00:56:35.605 --> 00:56:37.582
because I think we sort of just,

00:56:37.582 --> 00:56:39.123
and this is where I think

00:56:39.123 --> 00:56:41.992
what you were talking about
earlier, comes into play,

00:56:41.992 --> 00:56:45.145
with us being in this particular business,

00:56:45.145 --> 00:56:47.096
that we sort of move in the world

00:56:47.096 --> 00:56:51.340
and function day to
day within our company,

00:56:51.340 --> 00:56:54.320
as if we're also millennials, right?

00:56:54.320 --> 00:56:55.294
Right, yeah.

00:56:55.294 --> 00:56:58.429
We don't walk around like Generation Xers,

00:56:58.429 --> 00:56:59.564
whatever that would be,

00:56:59.564 --> 00:57:03.658
but I think that there's just this reality

00:57:03.658 --> 00:57:05.500
that probably comes out more

00:57:05.500 --> 00:57:07.120
in stuff like personal
discussions like this,

00:57:07.120 --> 00:57:09.725
which is we grew up without technology,

00:57:09.725 --> 00:57:12.214
we had to say, I'm gonna
meet you at 3:00 pm,

00:57:12.214 --> 00:57:13.264
at the graveyard

00:57:13.264 --> 00:57:16.250
and you just better be there at 3:00 pm,

